# Shell scripts MUST check out with LF endings, on every platform.
#
# These scripts are edited on Windows (core.autocrlf=true, so the working tree is CRLF)
# and executed on Debian. Git normalises on commit, so the `git pull` on a node has always
# been safe — which is exactly what made this dangerous: `git diff` looks clean, and
# `bash -n` passes under MSYS bash, so nothing on the authoring machine complains. The
# moment anyone takes the obvious shortcut for an urgent one-line fix and scp/rsyncs a
# script straight from Windows to a node, the shebang becomes `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` and
# Linux answers:
#
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# install-scripts.sh copies bytes (`install -m 755`) and filters nothing, so a CRLF file
# installs happily and only fails at run time. Pinning eol=lf makes the working tree match
# what actually runs, and removes the trap rather than documenting it.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the files that are read by tools on the Linux side.
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.j2    text eol=lf
*.cfg   text eol=lf
*.conf  text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
